<BaseLayout
  title="Whole30 – Rules, Food List & the Evidence | Nawtch"
  description="Whole30's 30-day elimination-and-reintroduction program: rules, food list, and an honest look at the evidence — including missing clinical trials."
  canonicalPath="/diets/whole30/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Whole30</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">A structured 30-day elimination-and-reintroduction reset, not a long-term diet.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/diets/">← Back to Diets</a>
  </header>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Is Whole30?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Whole30 is a strict, time-limited elimination program: for 30 days it cuts out added sugar, alcohol,
        grains, legumes, dairy, and certain additives to see how removing them affects how you feel. It's not
        meant to be followed indefinitely — after the 30 days, those food groups are reintroduced one at a
        time in a structured process designed to reveal how each one individually affects your energy, sleep,
        digestion, and mood. In that sense it functions less like a typical diet and more like a short-term
        self-experiment or reset.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Whole30 is a structured 30-day elimination protocol, not an ongoing diet. For 30 days it removes added
        sugar (real and artificial, including stevia, sucralose, and aspartame), alcohol, grains, legumes
        (including peanuts and soy), and dairy (ghee is an exception). It also has a rule nicknamed <strong>"SWYPO"</strong> ("Sex With Your Pants On" — the program's own deliberately memorable name),
        which flags compliant-ingredient junk-food mimics like paleo pancakes or almond-flour brownies: they're
        technically made of allowed ingredients, but the program considers them against the spirit of the reset
        because they recreate the psychological pull of treats it's trying to help you unlearn.
        <br /><br />
        After the 30 days, foods are reintroduced one group at a time over a minimum 10-day period, returning
        to elimination for 2–3 days between each to isolate how that food affects energy, sleep, digestion, and
        mood.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Rules have changed over time</strong> — worth knowing if you've seen older summaries: Whole30
        used to also exclude carrageenan, MSG, and added sulfites as food additives. It reversed all three of
        these exclusions (MSG in December 2021, carrageenan and sulfites in August 2024), citing insufficient
        evidence to justify banning them; the MSG reversal also specifically acknowledged the racially biased
        origins of the stigma against it. If a food list you've seen elsewhere still excludes these three,
        it's out of date.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Evidence</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Unlike every other entry on this page, <strong>Whole30 doesn't have independent, peer-reviewed
        clinical trials behind it.</strong> It's a company-created program, and after specifically checking for
        independent research on it, none exists: the only study of the Whole30 program by name is an
        unpublished, non-peer-reviewed pilot cohort of roughly 45 participants run by the company itself, plus
        self-reported alumni surveys. This isn't a gap we're softening — it's the single most important thing
        to know about this program before starting it.
      </p>
      <strong>Whole30's own claimed evidence:</strong>
      <a href="https://whole30.com/the-science-behind-whole30s-success/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Science Behind Whole30's Success</a>
      — self-reported, company-affiliated data, not independent clinical trials.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Independent assessment:</strong> Cleveland Clinic's dietitian-reviewed overview frames Whole30 as
      "an experiment," not an evidence-backed treatment, and notes the near-total absence of peer-reviewed
      research on it specifically.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://health.clevelandclinic.org/whole30-diet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read Cleveland Clinic's overview →</a>
      <br /><br />
      What <em>is</em> studied, separately from Whole30 itself, is the general elimination-then-reintroduction
      method it's built on — used clinically for identifying food allergies and sensitivities. That's a real,
      established diagnostic approach in its own right, but it's evidence for the general method, not proof
      that Whole30's specific 30-day version produces the health benefits it claims.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Food Examples</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Compliant:</strong> chicken, beef, pork, fish, shellfish, eggs; all vegetables (potatoes, leafy
        greens, peppers, squash); fruit; compliant fats (olive oil, coconut oil, avocado, ghee); some nuts and
        seeds (almonds, walnuts, cashews — not peanuts, which are a legume).
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Non-compliant:</strong> bread, pasta, rice, oats; beans, lentils, peanuts, soy and tofu; milk,
        cheese, yogurt; sugar, honey, maple syrup, artificial sweeteners; wine, beer, spirits; and SWYPO items
        like paleo pancakes or cauliflower pizza crust used as a treat substitute.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">A Sample Day</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Breakfast:</strong> scrambled eggs, sautéed spinach, avocado.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Lunch:</strong> grilled chicken over mixed greens with an olive oil-and-lemon dressing, roasted
        sweet potato.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Dinner:</strong> baked salmon, steamed broccoli, cauliflower rice.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Snack:</strong> apple with almond butter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who It Might Suit</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        May suit people who want a short, structured reset with a built-in process (reintroduction) for
        identifying personal food sensitivities. It's likely not well-suited to anyone with a history of
        disordered eating — Cleveland Clinic recommends provider clearance before starting for that reason —
        to vegetarians or vegans, since legumes and dairy are both excluded and leave few plant protein sources
        left standing, or to anyone looking for sustainable long-term structure: it's explicitly designed as a
        30-day experiment, not a lifestyle, and continuing it indefinitely risks nutrient gaps from cutting
        legumes, dairy, and soy without a plan to replace what they provide.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

